<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oswiu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 612–<a href="/years/670">670</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Oswy, Oswiu of Northumbria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oswiu was king of Bernicia from 642 and of all Northumbria from 654 until his death in 670. In 655 he defeated and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda of Mercia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-winwaed">Battle of the Winwaed</a>, briefly becoming overlord of much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In 664 he presided over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synod-of-whitby">Synod of Whitby</a>, where he chose Roman rather than Irish church practice. His marriage to Rhiainfellt may have brought Rheged into Northumbria.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Oswiu was born around 612, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> says he was 58 at his death. He was the third child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith of Bernicia</a>, and his mother was probably Acha, a princess of the royal line of Deira. In 616 Æthelfrith was defeated and killed at the battle of the River Idle, and his sons fled among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaels">Gaels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Ireland, remaining there until Edwin's death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hatfield-chase">Battle of Hatfield Chase</a> in 633. Oswiu went into exile aged four, and Bede writes that he was fluent in Old Irish and Irish in his faith.</p><p>His brother Oswald was killed in battle against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda of Mercia</a> at Maserfield, dated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> to 5 August 642, and the kingship passed to Oswiu. The Deiran nobility, who had accepted Oswald apparently on account of his mother, resisted Oswiu throughout his reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> says Oswiu held the throne for twenty-eight years with much trouble, harassed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda</a>, by his son Ealhfrith and by his cousin Æthelwald. Between 642 and 644 he married Eanflæd, Edwin's daughter. Around 651 he came into conflict with Oswine, the independent king of Deira, whose killing Bede blames on Oswiu; to expiate it he founded Gilling Abbey.</p><p>In 655 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda</a> invaded Bernicia with a large army. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> says Oswiu offered a great quantity of regalia and gifts as the price of peace, which Penda refused, and that Oswiu vowed to give his daughter Ælfflæd to the church and found twelve monasteries if victorious. At the Battle of the Winwæd, in the region of Leeds, Penda was killed along with King Æthelhere of the East Angles. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/historia-brittonum">Historia Brittonum</a> tells it differently, with the offer accepted at the siege of Iudeu. Oswiu became the dominant figure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, installing Ealhfrith as king of the Deirans and taking northern Mercia himself, but his domination lasted only about three years, until a Mercian revolt installed Penda's son Wulfhere.</p><p>In 664 at the synod of Whitby Oswiu accepted the usages of the Roman Church, and Bishop Colmán of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> departed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> writes that the dispute was brought to a head by Ealhfrith, who had adopted Roman usages at the urging of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Archbishop Theodore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> came north to meet Oswiu in 669. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> writes that Oswiu intended a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a>, but he fell ill and died, aged 58, on 15 February 670. His son Ecgfrith succeeded him in Bernicia, and he was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whitby-abbey">Whitby Abbey</a> beside Edwin of Deira. Alcuin, writing about a century later, called him very just, with equitable laws, unconquered in battle but trustworthy in peace.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oswiu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Penda's victories at Hatfield Chase in 633 and Maserfield in 642 made Mercia the dominant power in the Midlands, but his defeat and death at the hands of <b>Oswiu of Northumbria</b> at the Battle of the Winwaed in 655 led to three years of Northumbrian occupation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His brother <b>Oswiu</b>, king of Bernicia from 642, struggled with Deiran rivals, had King Oswine killed in 651 and finally made the union lasting after he defeated and killed Penda of Mercia at the Battle of the Winwaed in 655; the kingdom is dated here from that mid-650s…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Winwaed, probably near Leeds, <b>Oswiu of Northumbria</b> defeated and killed the pagan King Penda of Mercia, and many of Penda's allied kings also died.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-15">15 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Oswiu</b>, Bernicia and the Kingdom of Deira were joined permanently after 654, forming the Kingdom of Northumbria.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bernicia">Kingdom of Bernicia</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in 633 Deira had its own kings again, including Oswine, killed on the orders of <b>Oswiu</b> in 651.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-deira">Kingdom of Deira</a></li><li><blockquote>Rheged seems to have passed peacefully to Northumbria, perhaps through the marriage of <b>Oswiu</b> to Rhiainfellt, a descendant of Urien, around 638.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-rheged">Kingdom of Rheged</a></li></ul></section>
